course: dictionary
lesson: 14572
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the other words in the sentence, tells you which meaning the writer wants. «The guards pinioned the prisoner's arms so he could not move.» The guards and the prisoner tell you pinioned means held tightly.
explain: «The farmer pinioned the hen's wings so it could not fly over the fence.» Here the hen and the fence are the clues. Pinioned means cut or tied a bird's wing feathers so it cannot fly.
explain: «The eagle spread its great pinions and soared into the sky.» Pinions is a poetic word for wings. The eagle and soared are the clues. Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence? Then try it in the sentence to check.
fact: Pinion can be a verb (an action word) or a noun (a naming word).
fact: As a verb, «pinion» means to hold someone's arms tightly so they cannot move them, or to cut or tie a bird's wing feathers so it cannot fly.
fact: As a noun, «pinion» can mean a bird's wing, which is a poetic use, or a small gear wheel with teeth.
fact: In machines, a «rack and pinion» turns round movement into straight movement, as in a car's steering.
fact: Check the clues: arms, wings, feathers, sky or teeth tell you which meaning of pinion is used.
exercise: «The guards pinioned the prisoner's arms.» What does pinioned mean here? | To cut the feathers of a bird | *To hold someone's arms tightly so they cannot move | To spread a wing wide | To turn a small wheel with teeth
exercise: «The eagle spread its great pinions and soared into the sky.» What is a pinion here? | A pair of arms | *A bird's wing | A small gear wheel | A rope for tying boats
exercise: «The small pinion fitted into the toothed bar and turned it.» What is a pinion in this sentence? | A bird's feather | A prisoner's rope | *A small gear wheel with teeth | A loose stone
test: «The farmer pinioned the hen's wing feathers so it could not fly over the fence.» What does pinioned mean? | *Cut or tied a bird's wing feathers so it cannot fly | Held someone's arms tightly | Turned a large wheel | Painted a fence
test: Which sentence uses pinion as a noun? | The guard will pinion the thief | Please pinion the box shut | *The eagle's pinion was long and dark | The mechanic will pinion the wheel
exam: What does pinion mean in «The guard pinioned him against the wall»? | A rope for climbing | *To hold someone tightly so they cannot move | A bird's wing | A small toothed gear
